---
import Layout from '@/layouts/Layout.astro';
import PageMeta from '@/components/PageMeta.astro';
import Diagram from '@/components/Diagram.astro';
---

<Layout title="Evidence, provenance and qualified mappings" description="How attributed evidence and competency questions become traceable SPDTF candidates, and why five different mapping meanings remain qualified.">
  <PageMeta category="How we model SPDTF 2.0" variant="brand" updated="Evidence and traceability · mapping labels are qualified" />
  <h1>Evidence, provenance and qualified mappings</h1>
  <p class="lead">
    Every important candidate meaning should be traceable to a question, evidence,
    proposal, review and disposition. “Mapping” is always qualified because different
    records connect different objects and carry different authority.
  </p>

  <h2 id="questions">Competency questions organise evidence</h2>
  <p>
    Evidence is collected to answer a scoped business question, not simply because a file
    exists. A good competency question identifies the context, expected result and why the
    answer matters. Evidence may support, contradict or leave the question unresolved.
  </p>
  <table>
    <caption>Example question-to-evidence record</caption>
    <thead><tr><th scope="col">Field</th><th scope="col">Example</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><th scope="row">Question</th><td>Which source supports the legal title associated with this property?</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Context owner</th><td>Conveyancing, with a Property Data Services source dependency.</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Evidence</th><td>Participant scenario, title record, source metadata and PDTF 1.0 comparison.</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Candidate impact</th><td>Legal title resource, property relationship and source-evidence relationship.</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Open issue</th><td>Whether the source identifies the title assertion or only supplies a document.</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Test</th><td>A competency query can retrieve the property, title and attributed source.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2 id="traceability">Traceability from source to version</h2>
  <Diagram size="large" caption="A traceability receipt binds an attributed source to the question it informs, the exact candidate construct, its review and disposition, and the immutable version that includes the decision.">
flowchart LR
  accTitle: Evidence traceability receipt for a semantic candidate
  accDescr: An attributed source supports a competency question, which links to an exact candidate construct, review record, disposition and immutable candidate version.
  Source["Attributed source"]:::external
  Question["Competency question"]:::user
  Construct["Candidate construct"]:::process
  Review["Review and dissent"]:::user
  Disposition["Recorded disposition"]:::security
  Version["Immutable candidate version"]:::service
  Source --> Question
  Question --> Construct
  Construct --> Review
  Review --> Disposition
  Disposition --> Version
  </Diagram>
  <p>
    Machine extraction is recorded as a derivation, not as the source authority. If
    evidence conflicts, preserve the competing claims and ask the owner to dispose them.
    Do not manufacture consensus or collapse a source document, its claim and the
    real-world thing it describes into one resource.
  </p>

  <h2 id="mapping">Five qualified mapping meanings</h2>
  <table>
    <caption>These records must not share an unqualified “mapping” label</caption>
    <thead><tr><th scope="col">Qualified meaning</th><th scope="col">What it connects</th><th scope="col">Standing</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><th scope="row">PDTF 1.0 RML schema–ontology verification</th><td>Published schema structures and the PDTF 1.0-derived ontology.</td><td>Retained implementation and traceability evidence.</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Legacy JSON-LD context mapping</th><td>Legacy JSON property names and ontology terms for compatibility views.</td><td>Planned or retained migration work; not new semantic authority.</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">SPDTF 2.0 cross-context semantic mapping</th><td>Related but non-identical meanings at bounded-context boundaries.</td><td>Candidate agreement owned by Interoperability and reviewed by affected groups.</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Property Pack source-item coverage link</th><td>One of 451 required source items and one or more candidate constructs.</td><td>Coverage evidence only; <strong>not RML or R2RML</strong>.</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Deployment transformation runtime</th><td>Operational representations where a deployment needs a transform.</td><td>Optional implementation service; cannot decide semantic meaning.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2 id="strength">Mapping strength and identity</h2>
  <p>
    A mapping record names its relation, direction, purpose, justification, confidence,
    owner, source/target versions and review status. Exact identity is rare. Use
    <code>owl:sameAs</code> only when two identifiers denote the same thing in every
    relevant interpretation. SKOS mapping relations can express close, exact, broad,
    narrow or related concept correspondences, but their use also requires evidence.
  </p>
  <table>
    <caption>Choose sharing before mapping only when meaning is genuinely common</caption>
    <thead><tr><th scope="col">Situation</th><th scope="col">Preferred treatment</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><th scope="row">A governed external term exactly fits</th><td>Reuse the pinned term-level profile.</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Several contexts require one stable exchange meaning</th><td>Contribute a reviewed term to the small common boundary.</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Contexts retain different but related meanings</th><td>Keep both semantic homes and record a qualified mapping.</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Only a source field corresponds to a candidate construct</th><td>Record a coverage link, not an ontology identity mapping.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2 id="receipt">Evidence receipt</h2>
  <p>
    A candidate receipt identifies source, version, permission, sensitivity, context
    owner, question, proposed change, affected semantic outputs, review record,
    disposition, standards-profile version and validation scope. A PDTF 1.0 link must say
    whether it is evidence, corroboration, compatibility, comparison or supersession.
  </p>
  <p><a href="/spdtf-2/ontologies/validation">Next: validation, review and projections →</a></p>
</Layout>
